{% from "macros.j2" import q, plain with context %}
<style>
.lt{display:inline-block;width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;vertical-align:-2px;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08)}
.lt.green{background:#1baf7a}.lt.amber{background:#eda100}.lt.red{background:#e34948}.lt.grey{background:#d4d3cd}
table.sl td.c{text-align:center}
table.sl td.c .lt{cursor:help}
.sl-legend{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:14px;font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink2);margin:8px 0 12px}
.sl-card h3{margin:.1em 0 .3em}.sl-card dl{display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;gap:3px 12px;font-size:.84rem;margin:.4em 0}
.sl-card dt{color:var(--muted)}.sl-card dd{margin:0}
</style>
<section class="panel" id="tab-shortlist" data-name="shortlist">
{{ plain('shortlist') }}
<h2>Candidates for experimental validation {{ q('shortlist') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">The {{ SL.rows | length }} first-in-class genes of the Discovery tab, each checked against the questions a laboratory would ask before testing it, including what the UC and IBD papers on it say. A light per criterion: green meets it, amber partly, red fails it, grey not measured. Hover over a light for the values. The rows are ordered by points: green 2, amber 1, grey 0, red −1 (a null genetic test scores 0; the mouse column is not scored). Genes with equal points are equally supported. Every value comes from the other tabs; nothing here is a new estimate.</p>
<div class="sl-legend"><span><i class="lt green"></i> meets</span><span><i class="lt amber"></i> partly</span><span><i class="lt red"></i> fails</span><span><i class="lt grey"></i> not measured</span></div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>The criteria</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Reproducible:</strong> significant increase in at least 6 of 8 cohorts (amber 4–5), and the change holds after adjusting for colon segment, age and sex.</li>
<li><strong>Protein:</strong> the protein also rises in the independent proteomics study. Grey when mass spectrometry did not quantify it.</li>
<li><strong>Tissue change:</strong> more RNA per cell, or just more of the cells that make it? Two views: the bulk composition adjustment (lineage level) and the change inside the gene's own single-cell clusters, inflamed against healthy, which also sees shifts within a lineage such as crypt expansion. Green: the per-cell rise is at least 2-fold (3-fold when the bulk verdict is only partial); red: no per-cell rise (under 0.3 log2), so the single cells explain the increase by cell numbers. Checked on markers: DUOX2, MMP3 and CHI3L1 rise 16- to 140-fold per cell; TNF, IL1B and OSM do not, as expected for genes brought in by immune cells.</li>
<li><strong>Cell source:</strong> at least half of the gene's RNA comes from one cell family in inflamed single cells (amber 30–50 %), and the second single-cell atlas agrees (otherwise amber): you know which cell to test. Also amber: lining genes concentrated in the crypt zone (≥ 76 % of their epithelial RNA in stem, transit-amplifying and progenitor cells, halfway between EPCAM and the crypt markers LGR5 and OLFM4) that rise there. These may belong to the repair programme of the crypt, and blocking them could slow healing.</li>
<li><strong>Causal genetics:</strong> genetically higher level raises UC risk (green, supports inhibition) or lowers it (red); amber when tested without effect (it scores 0: a null test is not support); grey when no genetic instrument exists, which is the case for almost all new genes.</li>
<li><strong>Druggable:</strong> green with an antibody route (UniProt topology: an extracellular domain, a GPI anchor or secretion; a "membrane" location alone is not enough, because many proteins sit on its inner face) or strong small-molecule evidence in Open Targets (a high-quality ligand or pocket, a druggable family, a clinical drug). Amber with a pocket score ≥ 0.6 alone, a weak bucket, or a protein secreted but mainly in the ER. The pocket score alone ranked the UC small-molecule targets 4th to 25th of 45 proteins, so it does not decide.</li>
<li><strong>Safety:</strong> four flags: expression above 50 nTPM in vital organs, loss-of-function constraint, known safety events, and a Mendelian disease caused by loss of the gene (UniProt, susceptibility loci excluded). Green with none, amber with one, red with two or more.</li>
<li><strong>UC literature:</strong> every paper that names the gene with ulcerative colitis (or with colitis, IBD or Crohn's disease in the title or abstract) was read by a classifier agent: what kind of evidence (only in a list, expression, biomarker, genetic, functional) and, for functional studies, which direction. Each call is backed by a sentence copied from the paper and checked automatically. Green: a perturbation study shows a harmful role, so blocking it should help, and none shows the opposite. Red: perturbation studies show a protective role only, so blocking it may harm. Amber: a specific result on expression, a biomarker or genetics, or studies in both directions. Grey: no paper, or the gene is only named in a list of many genes or in a review. Only papers in a colitis or IBD setting count: of the papers that name these genes with UC anywhere in the text, most do so in a supplementary table or a list, or are about another disease. Functional results in other inflammatory settings are shown as context.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> whether the gene was ever perturbed in mouse colitis (information only; the literature review found two that were: RFTN1 and IKBIP. For the others a mouse model is the second experiment).</li></ul></div>
<div class="tw"><table class="sl"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Gene</th><th>Cell family</th><th class="num">Meta log2FC</th><th class="num">UC papers</th>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}<th>{{ label }}</th>{% endfor %}<th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in SL.rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td>{{ r.rank }}</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.lineage }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.meta_log2fc }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.uc_papers }}</td>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<td class="c"><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}" title="{{ label }}: {{ v[1] }}" aria-label="{{ label }}: {{ v[0] }}, {{ v[1] }}"></i></td>{% endfor %}
<td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>The first ten, with the proposed first experiment</h3>
<div class="grid">
{% for r in SL.rows[:10] %}
<div class="card sl-card"><h3>{{ r.rank }}. {{ r.gene }} <span class="small">{{ r.lineage }}</span></h3>
<dl>{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<dt><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}"></i> {{ label }}</dt><dd>{{ v[1] }}</dd>{% endfor %}</dl>
{% if r.papers %}<p class="small"><strong>Key papers:</strong></p><ul class="small">{% for p in r.papers %}<li>{% if p.pmid %}<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/{{ p.pmid }}/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID {{ p.pmid }}</a>{% else %}{{ p.pmcid }}{% endif %}{% if p.year %} ({{ p.year }}){% endif %}, {{ p.kind | replace('_', ' ') }}{% if p.direction in ('pathogenic', 'protective') %}, {{ p.direction }}{% endif %}: {{ p.summary }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>{% endif %}
<p class="small"><strong>Proposed:</strong> {{ r.experiment }}</p></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
<h3>Calibration: the same lights on known UC drug targets</h3>
<p>The {{ SL.cal | length }} UC clinical targets that are in the same gene table, scored with the same rules. Share of green lights:</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th class="num">First-in-class genes</th><th class="num">Clinical targets</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria[:-1] %}<tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td class="num">{{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class[k] * 100) | round | int }} %</td><td class="num">{{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets[k] * 100) | round | int }} %</td></tr>{% endfor %}
<tr><td><strong>Median points</strong></td><td class="num">{{ SL.summary.median_points_first_in_class }}</td><td class="num">{{ SL.summary.median_points_clinical_targets }}</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Clinical targets: {% for r in SL.cal %}{{ r.gene }} {{ r.score }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>What the calibration says</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Druggable</strong> passes every clinical target, as it should, and {{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class.druggable * 100) | round | int }} % of the new genes.</li>
<li><strong>Safety</strong> is green for only {{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets.safety * 100) | round | int }} % of the clinical targets against {{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class.safety * 100) | round | int }} % of the new genes. That is not because the new genes are safer: side effects and Mendelian diseases are known for genes that have been studied and drugged. A green safety light on a new gene means nothing is known, which is weaker than "safe". Many disease flags of the clinical targets are immunodeficiencies, the intended effect of an immunosuppressant.</li>
<li><strong>Tissue change</strong> is green for {{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets.tissue * 100) | round | int }} % of the clinical targets: several rise because immune cells arrive (IL23A, ICAM1), which is how an anti-inflammatory works. The first-in-class screen looks for a different kind of target, tissue that changes, so this light describes the kind of target rather than its odds of working.</li>
<li>No gene of either set has a green causal-genetics light: the genetic test is not where the targets of UC came from.</li></ul></div>
<div class="note"><strong>How to read this.</strong> No new gene meets every criterion: causal genetics is missing for almost all of them, because few genes have a genetic instrument, and only two have been tested in a mouse colitis model (RFTN1, IKBIP). In the colitis literature, two thirds of them are named only in gene lists or not at all: what supports them is this atlas. These are hypotheses ranked by data, to be tested in human cells first. The experiments are suggestions that follow from the cell source and the drug route; they are not designed protocols.</div>
</section>
